===== 45

Change 45 · compiler.md, “What the compiled path cannot compile yet”The code generator compiles a type assumption, but for four forms, and the call runs the assumed trait’s body
New text; underlined, what came in; nothing went
compiler.md, “What the compiled path cannot compile yet”, lines 53 to 60, a new paragraph and list after the forms of local function that the code generator refuses, and a sentence after the list
{+A type assumption e asif T has the value of e, but the call around it chooses its declaration as if e were a T, a supertype of e's type (Specification/basic/expressions/type-annotation.tex). The code generator compiles one at a dotted call's receiver, at a call of a functional method or an overloaded function whose every argument is an assumption, and elsewhere as the value of e. It refuses these forms (ledger row 818):+}

{+a call of a functional method or an overloaded function with an assumption at some of its arguments but not all: ‘Can't compile a dispatched call whose arguments are not all type assumptions’;+}
{+a generic method on an assumed receiver: ‘Can't compile a type assumption at the receiver of a generic method yet’;+}
{+a call of an overloaded generic function with an assumption at an argument: ‘Can't compile a type assumption at a call of an overloaded generic function yet’;+}
{+a call, at an assumption of a generic trait, of a method whose parameter has the trait's type parameter, as stamp(Q asif Tagged[\String\], s asif String) for stamp(self, x: T) in Tagged[\T\]: ‘Can't find the body that Tagged[\String\] gives stamp at ...’ (ledger row 1088).+}

{+At an assumption of a trait, the call runs the trait's body, also where the object overrides it.+}


Why, climb batch 33b. Not a new rule: a form that the code generator now refuses, and what a compiled call at an assumption of a trait runs. Climb batch 33b made the code generator find the method of an imported api’s trait by its api (F-391). So a call at an assumption of such a trait now runs the trait’s body, also where the object overrides it. Before, it ran the object’s override, a wrong answer by the specification. In one form the code generator now stops: a call, at an assumption of a generic trait, of a method whose parameter has the trait’s type parameter, as stamp(Q asif Tagged[\String\], s asif String) for stamp(self, x: T) in Tagged[\T\]: ‘Can’t find the body that Tagged[\String\] gives stamp at ...’ (row 1088). The list named three refused forms, and the part did not say which body runs. A worker would have met the fourth form with no sentence to name it, or expected the object’s override to run at an assumption. The list now names the fourth form, and a sentence after it says that the call runs the trait’s body. After the cold read, two fixes. The example of the fourth form showed only the declaration, so a worker could not tell which call is refused: it now gives the refused call from the ledger row. And the sentence on the trait’s body cited an entry of the record, to which this part gives no meaning: the citation is cut. The cold read also asked whether walk runs the trait’s body too. The skill writer left the sentence as it is, since the record does not settle walk’s answer for an object’s override. The point went to the coordinator. Change 168 gives what the sentence means for a comparison of strings.
===== 85

Change 85 · revival-changes.md, “Numbers”Seven number types are value objects in both libraries, and both paths refuse an object below one
New text; struck through, what went; underlined, what came in
revival-changes.md, “Numbers”, line 89, the paragraph on the flat number types, whole
The number types are flat siblings (P-43). {+In both libraries, seven are value objects (P-178): ZZ32, ZZ64, ZZ, NN32, NN64, RR32 and RR64. In the interpreter's library, QQ is a trait, and its values are the value object Ratio (P-166). The compiler's prelude declares no QQ. Do not extend a number type in a program. Declare a program's own number type below Number (P-160). Such a type runs on both paths. Both paths refuse an object declaration below one of the seven, as object Mine extends ZZ32. Walk refuses it at load: 'Attempt to extend object type ZZ32'. The compiled checker refuses it: 'Invalid type in extends clause: ZZ32'.+} The record calls this [-choice-]{+design+} route A[-. Route C is the way not chosen: a checker rule under which the number types could nest again. The record-]{+, and+} still calls the [-number and order traits-]{+number types+} “the tower”[-. That word does not mean that they nest.-]{+, though they do not nest.+}


Why, climb batch 33b. Neither a new rule nor a change of a rule’s meaning: a fact that the batch changed. Climb batch 33b declared the seven number types of the compiler’s prelude as value objects too, as the curator’s decision P-178 asks (F-388). The text said that the prelude still declares them as traits that a type can extend (row 1003), so that the checker accepts an object below one, and the object dies when its class loads (row 866). The batch fixed both rows. The text now says that both libraries declare the seven as value objects, that the prelude declares no QQ, and that both paths refuse an object declaration below one of the seven, each with its message: walk at load, and the compiled checker. A worker who read the old text would have expected such a program to compile, or written an XXX test for a defect that is gone. The orientation update made the change, and its judge said to install it. Its first wording said “an object”. The update’s own preservation check found that an object expression gets another message, ‘Objects can only extend traits.’, so the text now says “an object declaration”, the case that the compiled message is for. The judge noted that the sentence on the prelude’s QQ could open “Until the switch-over”, the form of a limit of the prelude, and left that to the next update. The skill’s cold read found nothing to fix here.
===== 87

Change 87 · revival-changes.md, the openingA sentence with a ledger row states a behaviour that is not settled, and which test asserts what
New text; struck through, what went; underlined, what came in
revival-changes.md, the opening, line 7, its third paragraph, its first two sentences, now four; the rest did not change
A sentence that cites a ledger row states a [-known departure of one path from a bold rule. A fix can change it, so do not assert it as correct in a test.-]{+behaviour that is not settled: a known departure from a rule, or a case that the rules do not decide yet. Either can change. Where the rules decide the case, assert their answer in an XXX test. Where they do not, assert today's behaviour in a plain test, one without the XXX prefix, and cite the row (tests-writing.md, “How a defect is recorded”).+}


Why, climb batch 33b. Neither a new rule nor a change of the rule’s meaning: two instructions in place of a ban and a sentence that read against it. The text told a worker not to assert such a behaviour as correct in a plain test, and two sentences later said that where the rules do not decide the case, a plain test asserts today’s behaviour beside the row. Read together, they seemed to forbid and to ask for the same test, and the judges of two earlier updates had noted it. The orientation update’s own cold read raised it, and the text now gives two instructions: where the rules decide the case, assert their answer in an XXX test; where they do not, assert today’s behaviour in a plain test, and cite the row. The judge checked them against tests-writing.md, “How a defect is recorded”, and found the rule the same: each case gets its one test, so the old ban holds without a sentence of its own. The skill’s cold read found that a worker may still pin today’s wrong answer in a plain test beside the XXX test, a test that breaks once the departure is fixed, and proposed to add “and assert today’s behaviour in no plain test”. The skill writer left the text as installed, since the sentence would undo a rewrite that the judge accepted. It names this as a decision not taken: restate the ban in a later orientation update, or keep the two cases as they stand. The point went to the coordinator.

